{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08 stable, freeze 2021-07-28; the later periodic update 2.08-2026-08-16 was not queried): all three named domain types are distinct families inside superfamily c.2.1 'NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains' (sunid 51735), which sits in fold c.2 (sunid 51734) and class c (sunid 51349, 'Alpha and beta proteins (a/b)'). The families are: c.2.1.1 'Alcohol dehydrogenase-like, C-terminal domain' (sunid 51736); c.2.1.8 'CoA-binding domain' (sunid 51900); and c.2.1.9 'Potassium channel NAD-binding domain' (sunid 63944, automatically mapped to Pfam PF02254). Each family's homology record reports homology_implied_by_level=true, homology_scope 'fa', and no warnings or heterogeneity. The superfamily homology record reports scope 'sf' with no warnings. The only annotation comment in these records is on c.2.1.1: 'N-terminal all-beta domain defines family'. Sibling families in c.2.1 include c.2.1.2 (Tyrosine-dependent oxidoreductases), c.2.1.3 (GAPDH-like N-terminal), c.2.1.7 (Aminoacid dehydrogenase-like C-terminal), and c.2.1.13 (Ornithine cyclodeaminase-like).

Interpretation: the deepest level SCOPe shares by all three is the superfamily c.2.1. Under SCOPe's rules, that supports probable common ancestry of these domains as members of one NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold superfamily. Because they fall in three different families, no pair shares a family, so SCOPe gives no close family-level relationship among them. A biologist should read the three as remote relatives that share a Rossmann-type dinucleotide-binding architecture and have diverged into distinct families. The family split is consistent with functional divergence, but SCOPe does not itself state the function of any of these families, so divergence of function is not verified here.

Limits and unsafe transfers: (1) SCOPe gives no substrate, cofactor, reaction, or ligand for any of the three families; the family names are descriptive labels. Do not transfer a function, cofactor, or reaction from one family to another, and do not treat c.2.1 placement as an interchangeable structural template. (2) The superfamily assertion is a superfamily-level homology claim, not a sequence-identity or structural-alignment result. I did not retrieve sequence identity, alignments, or structures, so I cannot state how similar any pair is at sequence or residue level; that requires external sequence or structural alignment evidence. (3) I used node-level family records and did not retrieve domain SIDs or boundaries, so this answer covers the hierarchy, not specific domain instances. (4) The c.2.1.1 comment 'N-terminal all-beta domain defines family' indicates that the family label depends on a neighboring region outside the Rossmann-like unit; the API does not expose the curator rationale, so I cannot explain it further. (5) From general background that was not retrieved here, NAD-binding modules of this kind bind NAD(H) or NADP(H) in medium-chain alcohol dehydrogenases and in potassium-channel regulatory domains, but the cofactor identity and role in each specific protein must be verified from sequence, structure, and literature before any functional claim. (6) Name searches for the three family names returned no results; an empty search is not evidence of absence, so I located the nodes through the superfamily children listing instead.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=alcohol%20dehydrogenase%20C-terminal&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=CoA-binding&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=potassium%20channel%20NAD-binding&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=dehydrog